# Original Codex rollout feasibility probe

This is a read-only, original-rollout probe. It does not start Codex, an app
server, or provider-session RPC. It reads bodyless BB thread metadata, then
only the server-filtered `thread/identity` and `turn/completed` event lanes.
The event request is bounded and rejects a saturated page; metadata, provider
identity, checkpoint, owner route, persistent-process snapshot, workspace
dirty-state identity, and rollout identity are fenced before and after reading.

The route is obtained only through `bun:sqlite` with `readonly:true,
create:false`: exactly one enabled, non-draining route and no migration claim
are required. The router process, management initializer, and status command
are not used. This host-bound probe uses the explicitly pinned Bun 1.3.14
helper at the selected host path only after checking its fixed SHA-256; an
ambient `PATH` helper is never accepted. The rollout path is opened with `O_NOFOLLOW`; its opened file
descriptor is resolved through `/proc/self/fd`, must be a bounded regular file
strictly beneath the real owner home, and is checked again after streaming.

The persisted native hook carrier is deliberately structural, following the
local [agent-evals normalizer](/home/ubuntu/phosphor/monorepo/tools/agent-evals/src/Normalize.ts:465): a `response_item` whose payload is a developer
`message`, whose metadata has a `hooks.additional_context` entry aligned with
an `input_text` content entry. The native message id and metadata `turn_id` are
the locators. Generic developer messages are never hook context. Hook text is
examined only transiently by an isolated, versioned provisional
`context-magnet-receipt/v1` parser, which checks its declared bytes, SHA-256,
and native turn identity before discarding the text. The eventual ETL contract,
not this probe, owns the durable receipt envelope.

The target rollout uses exact `session_meta.payload.cli_version: "0.153.2"`.
Native response-item metadata supplies the observed turn IDs; the completed BB
checkpoint must be one of them. UTF-8/JSONL parsing has finite byte, line,
record, turn, context, and wall-clock bounds. Unknown or malformed data, unsafe
or racing files, ambiguous ownership, partial BB state, or malformed native
carrier/envelope conflicts fail closed. A complete, structurally valid unwrapped
`hooks.additional_context` carrier instead reports the explicit
`unbound-hook-context` gap.

Live target `thr_urmi56uqw8` produced 133 records and 844550 bytes with zero
`hooks.additional_context` carriers, so its compact result is
`zero-exact-context-receipts`: no persisted hook context and no Context Magnet
receipt. Separately, the parent’s read-only Workbench inspection for the same
provider audience and completed turn found 34 complete observation records, all
with null content, zero original bytes, zero contribution ID, and zero outcome.
Together these are an observed/evaluated, no-contribution result. They prove
the Workbench context runtime observed/evaluated that audience and turn, but do
not prove that Codex emitted or persisted hook context, and they are not
evidence that a hook failed.
